 2. Exclusive: Anduril&#x27;s online arsenal 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 An EagleEye configuration, seen between then-Army Secretary Dan Driscoll and Anduril Industries' Chris Brose. Photo: Colin Demarest/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anduril Industries has quietly rolled out what it's calling Armory, a perusable database of drones , jammers, motors and other defense-tech products it has in hand, ready for sale and delivery.

 Why it matters: This is Anduril's e-commerce moment — one that could boost business-to-business sales and build supplier relationships for the neo-prime.

 "We're hoping we get a lot of original equipment manufacturer traffic and other types of A&amp;D companies coming through," chief business officer Matthew Steckman told me. "As a company, we've generally just gone after large, prime programs." How it works: You can browse Armory right now.

 Submitting an inquiry about any of the gear in stock — the Wisp sensor or Dive-LD undersea vehicle or Voyager chassis, for example — connects you to someone from Anduril to navigate the regulations and purchase. "We have the demand coming in — orders booked," Steckman said. "But we're always building to a slightly higher rate than the orders booked, because the history of our business has told us to do that." The intrigue: Missiles, like Barracuda, and other munitions were not listed on Armory as of last night.

 Follow the money: Anduril was this year valued north of $60 billion. Backers include Andreessen Horowitz , Founders Fund and Thrive Capital.

 The company is building the Fury robo-wingman for the U.S. Air Force, to fly alongside F-35s and F-47s, and recently expressed interest in the service's Massed Modular Aircraft effort. The bottom line: "Coming up with the name Armory? As you can imagine, so many debates. So, so, so many debates," Steckman said.

 "We're excited to see where it goes, and we're going to kick it into high gear." Go deeper: Luckey says Pentagon could have been "more forceful" against Anthropic 

 
 
