 3. Exclusive: Magnet-making moves 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Vulcan Elements will make magnets for the U.S. Army's SkyFoundry initiative, which seeks stateside mass production of small drones , CEO John Maslin told me.

 The big picture: Supply-chain surety is a national-security imperative. 

 China's rare-earth crackdown has strained manufacturers across the world while also complicating the relationship between Washington and Beijing. "We won because we're decoupled," Maslin said of his startup's selection. Zoom in: Vulcan will supply neodymium iron boron magnets for the service, which is closely watching the proliferation of unmanned battlefield technologies in Eastern Europe and the Middle East .

 The magnets will support production of brushless direct-current motors. "This is a major deal, if you believe drones are the future of warfare, delivery and agriculture," Maslin said. "Countries that make things win." Follow the money: This marks Vulcan's 10th Defense Department contract.

 Zoom out: Vulcan late last year secured a $620 million Office of Strategic Capital loan to "support the production of advanced rare earth element separation, metallization and magnet manufacturing capabilities."

 The White House intervened to make it possible, ProPublica reported . Vulcan responded yesterday to inquiries from the House Natural Resources and Oversight committees. A spokesperson separately told me that the startup maintains "it did not ask or solicit its investors to facilitate the Pentagon loan." What's next: Vulcan is building a 1.1-million-square-foot facility in Benson, North Carolina, about an hour from Fort Bragg .

 That footprint is equal to about 600 Waffle Houses, according to Maslin. "Making these magnets is not easy," he said. "It's actually phenomenally complex." Go deeper: Supply chain terror 

 
 
