 4. ICYMI: Saudi F-35s 
 
 By Barak Ravid and Colin Demarest 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 A U.S. Air Force F-35A catches the sun over Farnborough Airport. Photo: Colin Demarest/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The State Department approved the potential sale of 48 F-35 fighter jets to Saudi Arabia at an estimated cost of $24.3 billion.

 Why it matters: This major arms deal has been in the works for more than a year. If it goes through, Saudi Arabia would be the only Arab country that uses F-35s. 

 The Lockheed Martin-made warplanes would be a major technological upgrade for the Saudi Air Force and would raise questions about Israel's long-standing military advantage in the region. The deal must go through a review by Congress and could still get stuck. Even if approved, it would take years before the first jets were supplied to the Saudis. Driving the news: President Trump said before Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's visit to the White House in November that he approved of the deal.

 Since then, the State Department has reviewed and negotiated the proposal. What they're saying: "It's a categorically bad idea , until and unless the U.S. and [Saudi Arabia] agree to some concrete guardrails on Riyadh's defense and technological cooperation with China, more generally," Jonathan Ruhe, a fellow at the Jewish Institute for National Security of America, told Axios.

 The F-35, he added, is "a microcosm of a much larger problem in the relationship." Read the full story. 

 
 
