 5. Quick hits 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Lazaro Gamio/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 📉 Nuclear security conditions in the U.S. and Russia are "dramatically declining," driven by the suspension of international cooperation and weakened oversight regimes, including at the Nuclear Regulatory Commission, according to the NTI Nuclear Security Index.

 Why it matters: "That's concerning, because those two countries have the largest responsibility — globally — for nuclear security, given the size of their arsenals and their stockpiles," NTI CEO Christine Wormuth told me. 💭 My thought bubble: The report's most interesting finding may be about the emerging class of nuclear security leaders, which includes Australia, Ghana, Japan and the Netherlands. 🏗️ OpenWorks Engineering is opening a headquarters in Ashburn, Virginia, and planning a production facility in Detroit . The company, founded in the U.K., works on surveillance, counter-drone and other air-defense technologies.

 Why it matters: "This expansion is a natural next step in our mission to continue to protect the territory and airspace of NATO nations and their allies," CEO Chris Down said in a statement. 💭 My thought bubble: Northern Virginia's lovely this time of year. 🔭 Australian startup HEO raised $25 million. It raised $8 million in 2023. Backers include Beaten Zone Venture Partners and Dcode Capital.

 Why it matters: HEO uses "cameras and sensors attached to satellites to take 'flyby' images of other satellites and spacecraft in orbit," as my colleague Alex Fitzpatrick put it . 💭 My thought bubble: Space-domain awareness via startup? What a time to be alive. ⚙️ Lockheed Martin received its first batch of Patriot interceptor parts from GM Defense, less than a month after the two companies inked a contract.

 Why it matters: Anything that gets PAC-3 Missile Segment Enhancements going is a good thing. These parts generally "take months to years to produce," according to Lockheed. 💭 My thought bubble: Remember this ? 
 
